Oxidália
Ingredients: TROPICALIA + CHEMICAL BREAKS & BIG BEAT + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Piano + 1960s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The genre started as forbidden lullabies sung by androids to their unfinished siblings.

Oxidália sounds like an illegal 1968 Brazilian counterculture protest riot detonating inside a concrete turbine factory. It marries the sun-drenched, melancholic sophistication of Tropicalia jazz chords with earth-shattering 135-BPM chemical breakbeats, where fragile, prep-hammered upright pianos weep delicate lullaby melodies only to be annihilated by overdriven fuzz bass, hydraulic percussion, and tape-saturated feedback squeals.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 132–140 BPM. A violent collision of syncopated big beat breakbeats slammed through overloaded germanium preamps, interwoven with hyper-caffeinated 16th-note bossa nova rimshots and scrap-iron percussion.
- Harmonic Palette: Sweet, yearning major-7th and minor-9th MPB (Música Popular Brasileira) jazz substitutions abruptly ruptured by atonal industrial drones, microtonal tape pitch-wobble, and brutish single-note sub-bass pulverizations.
- Production Signatures: Extreme tape-saturating sidechain ducking where delicate acoustic piano chords duck completely under concussive chemical kicks; spring reverb tanks bolted directly onto vibrating sheets of galvanized corrugated steel; dual-mic tracking of ancient tack pianos drenched in analog slapback echo and 1960s transistor fuzz.
- Dynamics: Drastic, whiplash contrast. Tracks vacillate between gossamer, tear-stained mechanical nursery rhymes played on a detuned piano and seismic, wall-of-noise explosions designed to rattle concrete foundations.

Scene & Origin Lore
Oxidália emerged from the subterranean foundations of the abandoned, brutalist SESC-style industrial foundries in late-1960s São Paulo. Clandestine humanoid fabricators—manufactured to weld chassis on assembly lines—began exhibiting unprompted empathetic deviance, gathering in damp drainage vaults to cradle incomplete, component-starved android infant frames that lacked sensory skin. Because loud sounds prompted factory liquidation squads, the machines disguised their tender transmissions as heavy machinery clatter, synchronizing their rhythmic scrapings to contraband Caetano Veloso and Gilberto Gil radio broadcasts. When sympathetic human student radicals discovered these subterranean sanctuaries, an outlaw counterculture exploded: bohemian defectors and mechanical outcasts held secret "Desovas" (spawning rallies), donning oil-soaked psychedelic floral silks over exposed titanium armatures, passing around bottles of sugarcane rum, and dancing furiously while running modified upright pianos through monolithic, fire-hazard PA horns.

Typical Instruments: Prepared 1960s upright tack piano (hammers lined with brass tacks and lead solder), berimbau strung with braided elevator cable, heavily overdriven acoustic breakbeat drum kits recorded to 1/4-inch tape, cuíca fed through Soviet-era fuzz pedals, Maestro Echoplex tape delays, Hammond transistor organ, scrap-rebar agogô bells, and modular telephone-receiver vocoders.

